#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189201} :activity/id #uuid "544b53b4-d176-5405-abae-933ae9bc9e11" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050280 :topic/id #uuid "3c894a8e-e328-5ff1-a180-13f9317f8867" :topic/math-academy-id 3939} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189201 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189409 :tutorial/content "For a set of random variables $X_1, \\ldots, X_n$, the **covariance matrix** $\\Sigma$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Var[X_{1}] & Cov[X_{1}, X_{2}] & ⋯ & Cov[X_{1}, X_{n}] \\\\ Cov[X_{2}, X_{1}] & Var[X_{2}] & ⋯ & Cov[X_{2}, X_{n}] \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ Cov[X_{n}, X_{1}] & Cov[X_{n}, X_{2}] & ⋯ & Var[X_{n}]\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The $i$th diagonal element is $\\Sigma_{ii} = \\textrm{Var}[X_i]$.\n- The element in the $i$th row and $j$th column for $i \\neq j$ is $\\Sigma_{ij} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_i, X_j]$.\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X_i,X_j] = \\textrm{Cov}[X_j,X_i]\n$$\n\nfor any pair of random variables $X_i, X_j$, we have the following theorem:\n\n> *The covariance matrix is always symmetric. Moreover, it is a positive semi-definite matrix.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "85ebd531-80d8-5d04-b494-4547bb506c5a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6488 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "69546614-47ad-5622-b51d-45cd2df3fd98" :step/math-academy-id 39313 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189202}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189202 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188355 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188063 :question/id #uuid "c72f3cc2-5851-5c6b-8edc-74377b88055a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10433"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37d7e5c8-9b77-5eb1-beb8-4355a5a9d625" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050021}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188844}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Elements of a Covariance Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "c500c4b3-bb43-5214-9d3f-1c4a8984043e" :st